        <item>
            <key> <string>description</string> </key>
            <value> <string>Call/execute a Python Script from portal_skins and return its output.\n
\n
    ⚠ DESTRUCTIVE: executes server-side Python on the ERP5 instance with the\n
    connected user\'s permissions. The script can read, write, or delete any\n
    document the user has access to. Combined with erp5_skin_write this is\n
    arbitrary code execution — treat with the same care as a shell tool.\n
\n
    The script is called via acquisition — Zope finds it in portal_skins\n
    and executes it. If relative_url is provided, the script runs with\n
    `context` bound to that object.\n
\n
    USE THIS WHEN:\n
    - You want to test a Python Script after creating/updating it.\n
    - You need to run a script on a specific ERP5 object.\n
    - You want to call any acquired method on an object.\n
\n
    Examples:\n
    - Standalone: script_id="test_mcp_hello" → calls {site_root}/test_mcp_hello\n
    - On object: script_id="MyScript", relative_url="sale_order_module/1"\n
      → calls {site_root}/sale_order_module/1/MyScript\n
\n
    Failure is informative: a call that raises reports the detail plus the\n
    script\'s real stored Zope traceback when it failed to compile, along with\n
    a hint naming required params. A non-string return value is repr\'d.\n
\n
    Error ⇒ nothing persisted: on failure the request transaction is aborted\n
    before the error is returned (transaction_aborted: true) — not even\n
    mutations applied before the exception are kept. The raised error never\n
    escapes to the publisher, so this abort is what keeps a half-applied\n
    mutation from being committed (patch order is arbitrary on this Python 2\n
    instance — write idempotent helpers anyway).\n
\n
    Returns JSON with: script_id, relative_url, output (script\'s return value),\n
    and (on failure) error with the underlying traceback, transaction_aborted\n
    (always true on failure), plus an optional hint.\n
</string> </value>
